// Findings: worth a case officer's attention, but no reason to stop publication. A finding is a derived
// relation without @invariant (docs/concepts.md); each row has a proof like any other conclusion.

// How many applications a household has.
.decl application_count(household: symbol, n: number)
application_count(h, n) :- household(h), n = count : { application__household(_, h) }.

// A household with more than one application. The maximum of article 7 holds per application, so
// applications of one household are worth reading together.
.decl repeat_application(household: symbol, n: number)
repeat_application(h, n) :- application_count(h, n), n >= 2.

// An application whose award is below its entitlement: the maximum of article 7 cut in. It reads two results of
// calc/award.yaml, both known: `entitlement` (the cost share of articles 3 to 5 plus the allowance of article 6)
// and `award` (the entitlement, at most the parameter `maximum_award` of article 7, whose row the award's proof
// names). Only a known value is a fact for a rule, so an application whose award is unknown is not in it
// (docs/compiler/rules.md, "Calculation values in rules"). Worth a look before deciding: the household asked
// for more than the scheme pays.
.decl maximum_applied(application: symbol, entitlement: number, award: number)
maximum_applied(a, e, w) :- calc__entitlement(a, e, "known", ""), calc__award(a, w, "known", ""), w < e.

.output repeat_application
.output maximum_applied
